diff --git a/docs/mycelic/DECISIONS.md b/docs/mycelic/DECISIONS.md
index b267324..ef6df4b 100644
--- a/docs/mycelic/DECISIONS.md
+++ b/docs/mycelic/DECISIONS.md
@@ -231,3 +231,28 @@ A claim committed from evidence revised after the holder answered is born `stale
   last resort.
 - Sharded queries are about 2 to 3 times slower at p50 (shards are read one after another on one worker); write p95
   improves. The largest in-RAM vector matrix shrinks to the split shard's share, which is the limit splits exist for.
+
+## D18. The coordinator routes by hypergraph incidence: entity ids from any permitted record, hashed terms from public records only
+
+- Routing never widens authorization. `QuestionService.candidate_holders` (`Authorizer.can_route`) decides who may receive a question; the
+  router (`inquiry/routing.py:rank_holders`) only orders those holders and keeps `budget.holders` of them. Every score and every incidence
+  count is restricted to the authorized candidates.
+- **Entity index.** Holders publish content-free entity ids (services, components, symptoms, issues ...) from `public` records, and from
+  `members` records only when the owner opts in (`export_policy.entity_include_members`); never from private, restricted, confidential,
+  flagged or personal-domain records. An id needs 5 records unless the owner lowers `entity_min_records`. Off-switch `auto_entities`.
+- **Term index (H1).** A question that names a symptom phrase but no service has no entity to match. Holders therefore also publish keyed
+  hashes of the rare content words of their *public* records, `term:<hmac_sha256(tenant key, stem)[:16]>` (stems from the product
+  tokenizer, `mycelic/entities.py`), through the same heartbeat; the router hashes the question's words the same way and scores the overlap
+  (idf-squared weighted, at most 2 points against 3 for one entity hit). The key is per tenant (`OrgService.term_key`, derived from the server
+  secret): a copy of the coordination database cannot test a dictionary against it. Rarity: a word in more of a holder's records than 5 (and
+  than 10 % of them) is boilerplate and is not published; at most 1,000 ids per holder. Off-switch `export_policy.auto_terms`, cap `term_cap`.
+- **Decision: the publication threshold for terms is 1 record.** Term ids come only from *public* records, which every tenant member can already
+  read, so the index reveals nothing a member could not read; k-anonymity (the entity threshold of 5) protects nothing here and would hide exactly
+  the rare words that discriminate. Members, private, restricted, confidential and personal-domain records never contribute a term.
+- **Decision: only an authoritative snapshot retracts.** A heartbeat adds to what a holder has published (domains, entities, terms) but removes
+  nothing unless the holder vouches for its snapshot (`ingest.snapshot_complete`, true only when nothing is queued or leased). An empty or partial
+  first beat of a starting holder therefore cannot blank a holder that questions are being routed to. An owner switching a kind off, or revoking
+  a holder, withdraws at once in the same transaction.
+- Blind verification names the finding's entities, not its first words: the loop passes the entity display names to
+  `compose_verification_question` (numbers and tracker keys are never part of a topic), and the structured ids travel as `target_entities`,
+  which the router reads and a holder's owner never sees.
